On Fri, 13 May 2005, Cris Tuckman wrote:

> I'm curious about the specifics of pet training.  Any source divers out
> there who can tell me exactly when and how it happens?  I gather that giving
> tripe to your pets is required, but under what circumstance should this
> feeding occur?  And after it does, what is your pet more likely to do, and
> when?  Are they more likely to pick items up, or to move directly to you
> when they've picked something up, or to drop it at your feet after they've
> moved to you, or some combination of the three?

See the section concerning pets at:
http://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~eva/nethack/spoilerlist.html

Basically, you should feed them with tripe (carrot for horses). The sooner
after they droped an item, the better. The closer theitem was dropped to
you, the better.

So, with a bad pet, wait until he drop an item relatively close to you and
move to throw the tripe. With a good pet, wait until he drop an item just
in front of you (and don't move in the same instant) and throw the tripe.

It takes a bit of time but can be really rewarding the first time you
manage to empty a huge shop...

